<mujocoinclude>
    <asset>
        <material name="wooden_cover" texuniform="false" texture="wooden_cover"/>
        <texture type="2d" name="wooden_cover" file="../assets/kitchen-wood.png"/>

        <material name="dark_wooden_cover" texuniform="false" texture="dark_wooden_cover"/>
        <texture type="2d" name="dark_wooden_cover" file="../assets/dark-wood.png"/>

        <material name="plywood_cover" texuniform="false" texture="plywood_cover"/>
        <texture type="2d" name="plywood_cover" file="../assets/plywood-4k.png"/>
    </asset>
    <worldbody>
        <!-- ITER 34 (2026-05-18): table raised +10 cm per user (body z
             0.20 → 0.30). Top surface goes from world z=0.65 → 0.75.
             All keyframe contact-target z values + the object spawn z
             updated in lockstep below.
             2026-05-27: raised ANOTHER +10 cm (body z 0.30 -> 0.40,
             surface 0.75 -> 0.85) to reduce backward hip tilt in the
             brace (user hypothesis). Brace contact-target z 0.75->0.85
             / 0.80->0.90 across all 8 strategy JSONs + object keyframe z
             0.73->0.83 + object/target body z 0.83->0.93, in lockstep.
             2026-07-01: set to the REAL lab table = 87 cm. body z 0.32 ->
             0.42 (physical top face = body_z + 0.45 = 0.87; table_surface_pos
             sensor = body_z + 0.40 = 0.82). object/target z 0.85 -> 0.95
             (rest = surface 0.87 + object half-height 0.08). The gentler bow
             this affords is the "clean regime": upright lean, forearm flat,
             CoM back, reaching arm rides in the air.
             2026-07-01: briefly reverted to 0.77 to isolate a real STAND regression (it was NOT the
             table -- stand still failed at 0.77 with a fresh binary), then set BACK to 0.87 (body z
             0.42) to re-align with the 0.87-tuned forearm_brace keyframe. Real stand issue = the
             real-hardware base-velocity chain, not the model. -->
        <!-- 2026-07-25: RE-AIMED AT THE REAL ARPA BATTERY WORKCELL (user). The slab is now a
             copy of `CL_Assets/battery/arpa_system.xml`'s `battery_collision` proxy, resolved
             into robot-relative world coords exactly as `CL_Assets/battery/scene_h1_2_battery.xml`
             places it (arpa_cell pos 1.080 0.066 0, euler 0 0 -1.5708; battery_link
             0.067 -0.0235 -1.1869 under gantry_link z 1.9419, local euler 0 0 1.57, so the box
             comes out axis-aligned in world):
                 half-extents  0.6125 x 1.06 x 0.055   (2120 x 1225 x 110 mm pack)
                 TOP FACE      z = 0.810 + 0.055 = 0.865   (was 0.870)
             ★ STANDOFF IS A DEPLOYMENT CHOICE, NOT A FIDELITY NUMBER. The pack's DIMENSIONS
             and SURFACE HEIGHT are physical facts and are copied exactly. How far the robot
             stands from the pack is set by whoever positions the robot in the cell, so it is
             chosen here to preserve the validated brace geometry: centre x = 1.0125 so the
             NEAR EDGE stays at x 0.400, exactly where the 0.87-era brace was solved.
             Resolving the CL_Assets placement literally would put the centre at 1.0565 /
             near edge 0.444 -- i.e. the robot standing 44 mm farther back -- and that was
             MEASURED to break the brace outright: the `left_forearm_pad` capsule's REAR END
             sits at x 0.4157, so at a 0.444 edge 28 mm of the pad hangs over thin air. A
             strat-22 twin run then braced 2/1575 frames (0%) with the forearm hovering
             84-150 mm, versus 38% braced and a 0.03 deg flat hold at a 0.400 edge. Re-solving
             the ARM ALONE cannot recover it (7 arm joints cannot translate the shoulder; the
             solver's best was a rear end at 0.4284, still off-slab), so the fix is to stand
             the robot 44 mm closer -- trivially realistic, and it changes no physical
             property of the pack.
             ⚠ Measure pad seating on the capsule's ENDS, never its centre: the centre reads
             +61.8 mm onto the slab while the rear end reads +15.7 mm.
             `table_surface_pos` (framepos on the table_top GEOM FRAME) therefore now reads
             z 0.810, and `brace_gate_fix` derives the face as tsp_z + geom half-thickness, so it
             tracks this automatically -- do NOT hardcode 0.865 anywhere.
             NET EFFECT ON THE BRACE KEYFRAME: horizontally nothing changed (near edge held at
             0.400, pad centre +61.8 mm / rear end +15.7 mm onto the slab, as before). Vertically
             the pad now hovers +6.3 mm above the face instead of +1.3 mm, because the face
             dropped 5 mm. `brace_gate_fix` at a 6.3 mm gap is 0.995 -- essentially fully open --
             so the force gradient is intact and the planner just closes 5 mm more, which it
             already does dynamically. ⇒ the keyframe was NOT re-solved, and does not need to be.
             (The keyframe comment's "site x0.44" refers to the brace SITE, not the pad geom.)
             Legs are cosmetic (the real pack is gantry-borne) and were shortened to stand on the
             floor under the new 0.755 slab underside.
             ⚠ arpa_system.xml's own header says "battery surface ~0.73 m" -- that is STALE; its
             geoms and all 12 screw bodies put the mounting plane at 0.865. -->
        <!-- ★ TABLE X MOVED 1.0125 -> 0.78 (2026-07-29). MUST STAY IDENTICAL to the
             plant scene h1_mujoco/unitree_robots/h1_2/scene_handless_magpie_table.xml
             or the planner and twin disagree about where the slab is and every
             brace target is wrong.
             WHY: at x 1.0125 the slab's near edge sits at 0.400, but the robot spawns
             with its feet at x ~0.015 and cannot step in strat 22. Measured over 12
             runs, the left forearm pad only ever reached x 0.224-0.272 -- 128-176 mm
             SHORT of the edge -- so it was never over the slab and could not touch;
             only the wrist/gripper (x 0.397-0.441) reached, which is why they took
             all the load. Pad HEIGHT was already correct (0.904 vs 0.906 intended).
             0.78 puts the near edge at 0.168, ~70 mm inboard of the achieved pad x.
             ⚠ NOT the 456 mm that would reproduce the keyframe's assumed stance
             (feet 0.471): at that shift the near table LEGS (table_x-0.4 +-0.05)
             land inside the foot footprint and the toes would strike them. At 0.78
             the legs sit at 0.33..0.43 vs toes reaching ~0.195.
             Slab SIZE/FACE unchanged -> the ARPA battery reference (face z 0.865,
             thickness 0.11) is preserved; only its distance from the robot changes. -->
        <body name="table" pos="1.0900 0 0.5100">
            <!-- 2026-07-31 REAL ADJUSTABLE TABLE (CL_Assets lean_table parity): top
                 118 x 59.5 cm, SHORT side toward the robot -> half-size 0.59 x 0.2975.
                 Half-thickness stays 0.055 (real top is 4 cm; the thicker planner slab
                 only affects under-table clearance, conservatively) so the
                 body_z + 0.445 = surface formula lean_config.sh relies on is unchanged.
                 Near edge = body_x - 0.59; lean_config.sh table <X> [SURFACE_H] moves it. -->
            <geom name="table_top" type="box" pos="0 0 0.39" size="0.59 0.2975 0.055" class="visual" material="wooden_cover"/>
            <!-- ITER 28 (2026-05-18): solref 0.04 → 0.08 (doubled time-const
                 again from R9's 40 ms → 80 ms). User reported feet lift
                 BRIEFLY at the moment of arm contact in iter 27 — diagnosed
                 as an impact impulse spike from the arm slamming into the
                 table. With 80 ms time-const the impulse is spread over 5-6
                 MJPC control cycles (at 15 ms each) instead of 2-3, so the
                 contact force builds up gradually rather than peaking
                 instantly. Trade-off: slightly squishier contact feel, but
                 the user cares about feet staying planted more than rigid
                 contact response. priority=1 still wins over the hand side. -->
            <geom name="table_top_collision" type="box" pos="0 0 0.39" size="0.59 0.2975 0.055" class="collision"
                  priority="1" solref="0.08 1" solimp="0.85 0.95 0.001 0.5 2"/>
            <!-- legs: cosmetic support for the slab (the real pack is gantry-borne). Half-height
                 0.3775 at pos z -0.0425 spans world 0.000 .. 0.755, i.e. floor to the new slab
                 underside (0.810 - 0.055). Inset to +-0.4 x / +-0.9 y, inside the wider footprint. -->
            <geom name="table_leg_1" type="box" pos="0.53 0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.025 0.025 0.4225" class="visual"
                  material="dark_wooden_cover"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_2" type="box" pos="0.53 -0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.025 0.025 0.4225" class="visual"
                  material="dark_wooden_cover"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_3" type="box" pos="-0.53 0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.025 0.025 0.4225" class="visual"
                  material="dark_wooden_cover"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_4" type="box" pos="-0.53 -0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.025 0.025 0.4225" class="visual"
                  material="dark_wooden_cover"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_1_collision" type="box" pos="0.53 0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.03 0.03 0.4225" class="collision"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_2_collision" type="box" pos="0.53 -0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.03 0.03 0.4225" class="collision"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_3_collision" type="box" pos="-0.53 0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.03 0.03 0.4225" class="collision"/>
            <geom name="table_leg_4_collision" type="box" pos="-0.53 -0.2375 -0.0875" size="0.03 0.03 0.4225"
                  class="collision"/>
        </body>
        <!-- Object placed farther on table to require leaning -->
        <!-- Detachable object - child of world initially -->
        <body name="object_anchor" pos="0.9047 -0.2348 1.0350">
            <joint name="object_anchor_joint" type="free" limited="false"/>
            
            <body name="object" pos="0 0 0">
                <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="0.3" diaginertia="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
                <!-- Add contype/conaffinity to control collision -->
                <geom name="object" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.08" rgba="0.67 0.73 0.78 1" 
                    class="visual" contype="1" conaffinity="1"/>
                <geom name="object_collision" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.08" 
                    class="collision" contype="1" conaffinity="1"/>
            </body>
        </body>
        <!-- Target position visualization -->
        <body name="target" mocap="true" pos="0.9047 -0.2348 1.0350">
            <geom type="sphere" size="0.03" contype="0" conaffinity="0" rgba="0 1 0 .5"/>
        </body>
    </worldbody>

    <!-- ITER 26 (2026-05-18): REMOVED equality welds/connects entirely.
         R23 (welds) made the body sway-spring because a single SE(3) pin
         is over-constrained relative to a real foot's contact patch. R25
         (connects) was worse — a single-point pin gave the body a 1-DOF
         pivot it didn't actually have, body felt unnatural to user.
         Now relying on real physics:
           - gravcomp 0.97 → 0.90 in h1_2_pos.xml + h1_2_pos_hands.xml,
             giving net body weight ~49 N (was ~15 N) — friction × weight
             now provides meaningful foot anchoring without any constraint.
           - kp_ankle = 80 (from R12) handles the increased gravity load.
           - iter-22 soft anchor cost bumps stay as defense-in-depth
             (Foot Up 90, Left Leg Anchor 250, Right Foot Lift 180). -->
</mujocoinclude>